ABSTRACT = ("The rapid improvement of text-to-image generative models has made AI-generated "
            "images increasingly difficult to distinguish from real photographs, and detectors "
            "that are accurate on one generator family often degrade sharply when transferred to "
            "another. We present a Four-Branch Attention Fusion Network (FBFN) that classifies "
            "images as real or generated by combining four physically interpretable evidence "
            "branches—a CNN semantic branch, a ViT semantic branch, a noise branch based on "
            "Error Level Analysis and Steganalysis Rich Model filters, and a color branch based on "
            "Lab-space local entropy—through a channel-and-spatial dual attention fusion module "
            "followed by global pooling. Trained on a Stable Diffusion v1.4 subset and evaluated "
            "across eight generators under the GenImage protocol, FBFN attains an area under the "
            "ROC curve (AUC) above 99% on all Stable Diffusion-family generators, matching a "
            "stronger recent black-box detector on this family while retaining pixel-level "
            "interpretable evidence. Ablation experiments reveal that the noise branch is "
            "near-redundant within the training distribution but is specifically responsible for "
            "cross-generator generalization, degrading AUC by 5–7 points on distant generators "
            "when removed. Multi-generator joint training further recovers most of the cross-family "
            "gap. We discuss where the method remains weak—notably on GAN-generated images—and "
            "position its interpretability as the principal distinction from stronger bit-plane "
            "detectors.")

KEYWORDS = ("AI-generated image detection; forgery detection; interpretable deep learning; "
            "cross-generator generalization; attention fusion.")

